---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RandomStringGenerator from '@/components/tools/security/RandomStringGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="random-string-generator">
  <RandomStringGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate random strings</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Choose a character set. Alphanumeric is the safe default: it passes through URLs, filenames,
        shell arguments and spreadsheets unchanged. Pick hex if the value sits next to hashes, base64url
        if it goes in a URL, and digits if people will read it aloud.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the length. The panel shows the bits per character for the alphabet you chose, so you can
        work back from a target: 128 bits of base64url is 22 characters, 128 bits of hex is 32.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add a prefix or suffix if you want the values to show what they are. Both are fixed text and add
        no entropy, and the tool says so.
      </li>
      <li>
        Generate in bulk: up to 500 at a time. Copy them as a block or download a plain text file with one
        per line.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Bits per character, by alphabet</h2>
    <p>
      Entropy is length multiplied by the base-2 logarithm of the alphabet size. The last two columns give
      the length needed for the two thresholds used in practice.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Character set</th>
          <th>Size</th>
          <th>Bits per character</th>
          <th>Length for 128 bits</th>
          <th>Length for 256 bits</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Digits (0–9)</td><td>10</td><td>3.32</td><td>39</td><td>78</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hexadecimal</td><td>16</td><td>4.00</td><td>32</td><td>64</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lowercase alphanumeric</td><td>36</td><td>5.17</td><td>25</td><td>50</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mixed-case alphanumeric</td><td>62</td><td>5.95</td><td>22</td><td>44</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Base64URL</td><td>64</td><td>6.00</td><td>22</td><td>43</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Printable ASCII</td><td>94</td><td>6.55</td><td>20</td><td>40</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Uses for random strings</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        API keys and access tokens: 32 base64url characters is 192 bits, far past any brute-force risk.
        Add a prefix (<code>sk_live_</code>, <code>pk_test_</code>) so people can recognise them in logs and
        scanning tools can identify a leaked key.
      </li>
      <li>
        Session identifiers and CSRF tokens: OWASP asks for at least 128 bits from a cryptographically
        secure source, which this page gives.
      </li>
      <li>
        Database seed data and test fixtures: bulk mode with a prefix gives a few hundred unique,
        obviously fake values in one click.
      </li>
      <li>
        Coupon, invite and licence codes: use the uppercase alphanumeric set without ambiguous characters,
        so people can read a code over the phone without a spelling alphabet.
      </li>
      <li>
        Salts and nonces you handle by hand: hex is usually the format the other tools expect.
      </li>
      <li>
        Temporary filenames and cache-busting suffixes: lowercase alphanumeric avoids case-sensitivity
        problems across operating systems.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why the randomness source matters</h2>
    <p>
      Some random string pages use <code>Math.random</code>. That function is fast and predictable: a
      modest number of consecutive outputs is enough to reconstruct its internal state, and then all
      future values are known. That is acceptable for a dice animation and a vulnerability for an API key.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page uses <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, which draws on the operating system's entropy
      pool, the same source a server-side library uses. It also avoids a smaller problem: a plain
      <code>%</code> that turns a 32-bit random number into an index makes the first characters of the
      alphabet slightly more likely than the rest, because 2<sup>32</sup> is not divisible by 62. The tool
      discards and redraws values that land in the last partial bucket, so each character is exactly
      equally likely.
    </p>

    <h3>Custom alphabets and duplicate characters</h3>
    <p>
      If you give your own character set, the tool removes duplicate characters before it generates. A
      character listed twice would appear twice as often and reduce the real entropy below the figure on
      the page. With duplicates removed, the alphabet size and the bits-per-character number stay correct.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a length</h2>
    <p>
      For a credential, 128 bits is the standard minimum and 256 bits is the safe margin. For values that
      must be unique but need not be secret, such as a cache key or a filename, 64 bits makes a collision
      very unlikely. When a person must type the value, trade length against the alphabet: 12 characters of
      uppercase alphanumeric without ambiguous glyphs is still 60 bits, and causes fewer transcription
      errors than a shorter dense string.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
